== Relative $K$-Theory <sec:relative-k-theory>

Let $J$ be a two-sided ideal of the ring $A$, and let $overline(A) = A slash J$.
The surjection $A -> overline(A)$ induces maps
$
  K_1 lr((A)) -> K_1 lr((overline(A))), quad
  K_0 lr((A)) -> K_0 lr((overline(A))),
$
and we wish to compare the $K$-theory of $A$ with that of $overline(A)$. As
pointed out in @rem:swan-relative-grothendieck-group, there is an exact sequence

#source(137)
$
  K_1 lr((A)) -> K_1 lr((overline(A))) -> K_0 lr((A,J))
  -> K_0 lr((A)) -> K_0 lr((overline(A)))
$
<eq:relative-grothendieck-comparison>
#idx("Relative K₀-group", target: [@eq:relative-grothendieck-comparison])
involving $K_0 lr((A,J))$, the relative $K_0$-group of $A$ relative to $J$. We
shall see here that the sequence can be extended to the left by a relative
$K_1 lr((A,J))$. Later, in §@sec:milnor-k2, we shall obtain a further extension
to the left by means of $K_2$ terms.

The relative group $K_0 lr((A,J))$, as described in
@rem:swan-relative-grothendieck-group, is generated by expressions $[M,f,N]$
with $M,N in cal(P)(A)$ and where
$f: overline(A) times.o_A M tilde.equiv overline(A) times.o_A N$. These
expressions satisfy certain defining relations, which we now recall. For each
$M in cal(P)(A)$, put
$overline(M) = M slash J M tilde.equiv overline(A) times.o_A M$. Then:

#enum(
  numbering: "(i)",
  [Given $L,M,N in cal(P)(A)$ and $overline(A)$-isomorphisms
    $f: overline(L) tilde.equiv overline(M)$,
    $g: overline(M) tilde.equiv overline(N)$, we have
    $ [L,g f,N] = [L,f,M] + [M,g,N]. $],
  [Given $A$-exact sequences
    $
      0 -> M_1 -> M_2 -> M_3 -> 0, quad
      0 -> N_1 -> N_2 -> N_3 -> 0, quad
      "where" M_i,N_i in cal(P)(A),
    $
    and given $overline(A)$-isomorphisms
    $f_i: overline(M_i) tilde.equiv overline(N_i)$ ($i = 1,2,3$) which commute
    with the maps associated with the given sequences, we have
    $ [M_2,f_2,N_2] = [M_1,f_1,N_1] + [M_3,f_3,N_3]. $],
)

The map $K_1 lr((overline(A))) -> K_0 lr((A,J))$ assigns to each
$f in GL_n lr((overline(A)))$ the triple $[A^n,f,A^n] in K_0 lr((A,J))$.
Further, the map $K_0 lr((A,J)) -> K_0 lr((A))$ carries $[M,f,N]$ onto
$[M]-[N]$, for $M,N in cal(P)(A)$ and $f: overline(M) tilde.equiv overline(N)$.

Now let $A subset.eq B$ be an inclusion of rings, and let $J$ be a two-sided
ideal of $B$ contained in $A$. Setting $overline(A) = A slash J$,
$overline(B) = B slash J$, we have $overline(A) subset.eq overline(B)$, and
there is a commutative diagram with exact rows
#numbered-block(relative-excision-comparison)
<eq:relative-excision-comparison>
We shall now prove the Excision Theorem, which states that the map $theta$ is an
isomorphism. The authors wish to thank H. Bass for supplying the proof, as well
as for many other helpful comments on this chapter.

#theorem(name: "Excision Theorem")[
  Let $A$ be a subring of $B$, and let $J$ be a two-sided ideal of $B$ contained
  in $A$. Then the map $B times.o_A ast$ gives an isomorphism
  $ theta: K_0 lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv K_0 lr((B,J)). $
] <th:relative-grothendieck-excision>
#idx("Excision Theorem", target: [@th:relative-grothendieck-excision])
#idx("Relative K-theory", target: [@sec:relative-k-theory])

#proof[
  Each $M in cal(P)(A)$ will be identified with its image $1 times.o M$ in
  $B times.o_A M$, and the tensor product will be written as $B M$.

  #source(138)
  Note that $J B M = J M$, so
  $
    overline(B M) = B M slash J B M = B M slash J M
    = overline(B) overline(M),
  $
  where bars denote reduction mod $J$.

  To prove $theta$ surjective, let $x = [P,f,Q] in K_0 lr((B,J))$, where
  $P,Q in cal(P)(B)$ and $f: overline(P) tilde.equiv overline(Q)$. Now
  $ x = [P plus.o P', f plus.o 1,Q plus.o P'] $
  for $P' in cal(P)(B)$. Choose $P'$ so that $P plus.o P'$ is $B$-free. Changing
  notation, we may now assume that $P$ is $B$-free, and so $P = B M$ for some
  free $A$-module $M$. Define $N$ as the pullback of the maps
  $Q -> overline(Q)$,
  $overline(M) -> overline(B) overline(M) tilde.equiv overline(Q)$. Then
  $N in cal(P)(A)$ by @th:milnor-projective-patching. In the commutative diagram
  #relative-excision-pullback
  the map $i$ is injective, $h$ is surjective, and $ker h tilde.equiv J Q$ (see
  Exercise @i:exc:pullback-kernel). Viewing $N$ as an $A$-submodule of $Q$, we
  have $J Q subset.eq N subset.eq Q$. Since
  $h(B N) = overline(B) overline(M) tilde.equiv overline(Q)$, we obtain
  $Q = B N + J Q = B N$, and then $J Q = J N$. Thus $h$ induces an
  $overline(A)$-isomorphism $g: overline(N) tilde.equiv overline(M)$, and
  $g^(-1) = f$. It follows that $x = theta [M,g^(-1),N]$, and therefore $theta$
  is surjective.

  Turning to the proof that $theta$ is injective, let
  $x = [M,f,N] in ker theta$, where $M,N in cal(P)(A)$ and
  $f: overline(M) tilde.equiv overline(N)$. As above, we may assume that $M$ is
  $A$-free. If $f$ can be lifted to an $A$-isomorphism $phi: M tilde.equiv N$,
  then by the discussion preceding @eq:relative-triple-short-exact-sequence
  there is an isomorphism of triples
  $ (phi,1): (M,f,N) tilde.equiv (N,id_overline(N),N). $
  Then $x = [N,id,N] = 0$ in $K_0 lr((A,J))$, as desired. We must still show how
  to lift $f$.

  Let $f$ induce $f^ast: overline(B M) tilde.equiv overline(B N)$; then
  $ 0 = theta(x) = [B M,f^ast,B N] in K_0 lr((B,J)). $
  The map $K_0 lr((B,J)) -> K_0 lr((B))$ carries the above triple to
  $[B M]-[B N]$, so $B M$ is stably isomorphic to $B N$. Replacing $x$ by
  $x+[F,id,F]$ with $F$ $A$-free, and changing notation, we may assume hereafter
  that $B M = B N = V$, say, where $V$ is $B$-free of rank $r$. We now view $M$
  and $N$ as $A$-submodules of $V$ such that
  $
    B M = B N = V, quad J M = J N = J V, quad
    overline(M) = M slash J M subset.eq V slash J V = overline(V),
  $

  #source(139)
  and likewise $overline(N) subset.eq overline(V)$. Thus there is a commutative
  diagram
  #relative-excision-inclusions
  where the vertical maps are inclusions, and where
  $f^ast in Aut_overline(B) overline(V) = GL_r lr((overline(B)))$. Viewing
  $f^ast$ as representing an element of $K_1 lr((overline(B)))$, its image in
  $K_0 lr((B,J))$ is the zero element $[V,f^ast,V]$, so by
  @eq:relative-grothendieck-comparison with $A$ replaced by $B$, there exists an
  element $g in GL lr((B))$ with image $f^ast$ in $K_1 lr((overline(B)))$.
  Stabilizing and changing notation once more, we may assume that $g in Aut_B V$
  induces the map $f^ast in Aut_overline(B) overline(V)$.

  Now consider the diagrams
  #relative-excision-lift
  where $f^ast = overline(g)$. Since
  $overline(g)(overline(M)) = f(overline(M)) = overline(N)$, we obtain
  $g(M) = N+J V$, so $g(M) = N$. Also, $g$ is injective on $V$, hence also on
  $M$, so $g|_M$ is the desired lifting of $f$, and the proof is complete.
]

(See Remark @rem:relative-whitehead-excision below, concerning the analogue of
@th:relative-grothendieck-excision for $K_1$.)

Just as in homological algebra, many computations in algebraic $K$-theory depend
on the existence of various types of exact sequences: localization,
Mayer–Vietoris, and relative $K$-theory sequences. We show next how to extend
@eq:relative-grothendieck-comparison one term to the left, by means of a
relative group $K_1 lr((A,J))$, defined below.

Let $overline(A) = A slash J$, where $J$ is a two-sided ideal of the ring $A$.
There is a homomorphism $GL_n lr((A)) -> GL_n lr((overline(A)))$ for each $n$,
defined by reduction mod $J$. Let $GL_n lr((A,J))$ be the kernel, so there is an
exact sequence
$ 1 -> GL_n lr((A,J)) -> GL_n lr((A)) -> GL_n lr((overline(A))). $
<eq:relative-general-linear-sequence>
#idx("Relative K-theory", target: [@eq:relative-general-linear-sequence])
Now set
$ GL lr((A,J)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r GL_n lr((A,J)) ⊴ GL lr((A)). $
Now let $lr({E_(i j) lr((a))})$ be the elementary matrices defined in
@eq:elementary-matrix-definition. For each $n >= 1$, let $E_n lr((A))$ be the
subgroup of $GL_n lr((A))$ generated by elementary matrices. Further, set
$
  E_n lr((A,J)) = & "normal subgroup of" E_n lr((A)) "generated by" \
                  & lr({E_(i j) lr((a)): a in J, 1 <= i,j <= n}).
$
<eq:relative-elementary-subgroup>

#source(140)
Put
$
  E lr((A)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r E_n lr((A)), quad
  E lr((A,J)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r E_n lr((A,J)),
$
so $E lr((A,J)) subset.eq GL lr((A,J))$. We shall show below that
$E lr((A,J)) ⊴ GL lr((A,J))$; taking this for granted for the moment, let us
define
$ K_1 lr((A,J)) = GL lr((A,J)) slash E lr((A,J)). $
<eq:relative-whitehead-group>
#nidx(
  "$K_1 lr((A,J))$",
  "relative K-group",
  target: [@eq:relative-whitehead-group],
)
#idx("Relative K₁-group", target: [@eq:relative-whitehead-group])
Note that $K_1 lr((A,A)) = K_1 lr((A))$, while $K_1 lr((A,0))$ is trivial.

#theorem(title: [Bass])[
  Let $J$ be a two-sided ideal of $A$. Then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$E lr((A,J)) = [E lr((A)),E lr((A,J))]
      = [GL lr((A)),GL lr((A,J))] ⊴ GL lr((A))$.],
    [The group $K_1 lr((A,J))$ is abelian, and equals the center of
      $GL lr((A)) slash E lr((A,J))$.],
  )
] <th:bass-relative-elementary-commutators>

#proof[
  We use the formulas @eq:steinberg-elementary-relations. For $i,j,k$ distinct,
  we have
  $ [E_(i k) lr((1)),E_(k j) lr((a))] = E_(i j) lr((a)), quad a in A. $
  Choosing $a in J$, this gives
  $ E_(i j) lr((a)) in [E lr((A)),E lr((A,J))] ⊴ E lr((A)). $
  Therefore
  $
    E lr((A,J)) = [E lr((A)),E lr((A,J))]
    subset.eq [GL lr((A)),GL lr((A,J))],
  $
  and we must prove that equality holds. Let
  $
    bold(x) in GL_n lr((A)), quad bold(y) in GL_n lr((A,J)), quad bold(y) =
    1+bold(z),
  $
  where $bold(z)$ has entries in $J$. It is easily checked that #named-equation(
    name: [$(ast)$],
  )[$
    mat(bold(y) bold(x), 0; 0, 1) mat(1, (bold(y) bold(x))^(-1) bold(z); 0, 1)
    bold(P)
    mat(1, 0; -bold(y)^(-1) bold(z) bold(x), 1) = mat(bold(x), 0; 0, bold(y)),
  $] <eq:relative-commutator-block-identity>
  where
  $
    bold(P) = mat(1, 0; -bold(x), 1) mat(1, -bold(x)^(-1) bold(z); 0, 1) mat(
      1,
      0; bold(x), 1
    ).
  $
  Then $bold(P) in E_(2 n) lr((A,J))$ since
  $E_(2 n) lr((A,J)) ⊴ E_(2 n) lr((A))$. Thus
  @eq:relative-commutator-block-identity gives
  $
    mat(bold(y) bold(x), 0; 0, 1) in mat(bold(x), 0; 0, bold(y)) E_(2 n)
    lr((A,J)).
  $

  #source(141)
  For $bold(x) = 1$, this yields
  $mat(bold(y), 0; 0, bold(y)^(-1)) in E_(2 n) lr((A,J))$ for all
  $bold(y) in GL_n lr((A,J))$. Further, letting $bold(x) in GL_n lr((A))$ be
  arbitrary, we obtain
  $
    mat(bold(x) bold(y), 0; 0, 1) = mat(bold(x), 0; 0, bold(y)) mat(
      bold(y), 0;
      0, bold(y)^(-1)
    )
    in mat(bold(x), 0; 0, bold(y)) E_(2 n) lr((A,J)),
  $
  and so
  $
    mat(bold(x) bold(y) bold(x)^(-1) bold(y)^(-1), 0; 0, 1)
    &= mat(bold(x), 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(y) bold(x)^(-1) bold(y)^(-1), 0; 0, 1) \
    &in mat(bold(x), 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(x)^(-1) bold(y)^(-1), 0; 0, bold(y)) E_(2
    n) lr((A,J)) \
    &subset.eq E_(2 n) lr((A,J)).
  $
  This shows that $[GL lr((A)),GL lr((A,J))] subset.eq E lr((A,J))$, and
  establishes (i).

  To prove (ii), observe first that $K_1 lr((A,J))$ is abelian since
  $E lr((A,J))$ contains the commutator subgroup of $GL lr((A,J))$. Further, if
  $u$ lies in the center of $GL lr((A)) slash E lr((A,J))$, its image
  $overline(u) in GL lr((A)) slash GL lr((A,J))$ is also central. This latter
  quotient is a subgroup of $GL lr((overline(A)))$ containing
  $E lr((overline(A)))$. But the centralizer of $E lr((overline(A)))$ in
  $GL lr((overline(A)))$ is $1$, so $overline(u) = 1$ and therefore
  $u in GL lr((A,J))$. This completes the proof of the theorem.
]

#corollary[
  Let $J$ be a two-sided ideal of the ring $A$, and let $K_1 lr((A,J))$ be
  defined as above. Then there is an exact sequence
  $
    K_1 lr((A,J)) -> K_1 lr((A)) -> K_1 lr((overline(A)))
    -> K_0 lr((A,J)) -> K_0 lr((A)) -> K_0 lr((overline(A))).
  $
] <cor:relative-k-theory-exact-sequence>

#proof[
  It suffices to prove exactness at $K_1 lr((A))$. Consider the commutative
  diagram with exact rows:
  #relative-elementary-kernel
  The vertical maps are inclusions, so the Snake Lemma for Groups (see Exercise
  @exc:snake-lemma-groups) gives an exact sequence
  $
    1 -> GL lr((A,J)) slash (E lr((A)) inter GL lr((A,J)))
    -> K_1 lr((A)) -> K_1 lr((overline(A))).
  $
  Since $E lr((A,J)) subset.eq E lr((A)) inter GL lr((A,J))$, it follows from
  the above and formula @eq:relative-whitehead-group that
  $ K_1 lr((A,J)) -> K_1 lr((A)) -> K_1 lr((overline(A))) $
  is exact. This completes the proof.
]

We now give another approach to relative $K$-theory, due to Bass and Stein,
which is based on the use of an appropriate Mayer–Vietoris sequence.

#source(142)
The advantages of this approach are its simplicity, and the fact that it extends
readily to the $K_2$ terms. However, the description of the relative groups
$K_0 lr((A,J))$ and $K_1 lr((A,J))$ is not so intuitively satisfying. We follow
the treatment in Milnor’s book @bib:Milnor1971.

As above, let $overline(A) = A slash J$, where $J$ is a two-sided ideal of $A$.
The _double of $A$ along $J$_ is the ring $D$ defined by the fiber product
#numbered-block(relative-double-square)
<eq:relative-double-fiber-product>
#idx(
  "Double (of a ring relative to ideal)",
  target: [@eq:relative-double-fiber-product],
)
where $f' = g'$ is the canonical surjection $A -> overline(A)$. Thus,
$
  D = lr(
    {(x,y) in A plus.o A: overline(x) = overline(y)
      "in" overline(A)}
  ).
$
The Mayer–Vietoris sequence @eq:mayer-vietoris-sequence gives us an exact
sequence
$
  K_1 lr((D)) arrow.r^((f,g)) K_1 lr((A)) times K_1 lr((A))
  arrow.r^((f',1 slash g')) K_1 lr((overline(A))) \
  arrow.r^partial K_0 lr((D)) arrow.r^((f,g))
  K_0 lr((A)) plus.o K_0 lr((A)) arrow.r^((f',-g'))
  K_0 lr((overline(A))).
$
<eq:double-mayer-vietoris-sequence>
(The map $(f',1 slash g')$ is defined by
$
  (f',1 slash g')(x,y) = f'(x) slash g'(y)
  quad "for" x,y in K_1 lr((A)). )
$
We note that
$
  im(f', 1 slash g') = im g' "in" K_1 lr((overline(A))), quad
  im(f', -g') = im g' "in" K_0 lr((overline(A))).
$
Now let $x in K_1 lr((A))$; then
$
  x in ker g' & <=> (0,x) in ker(f', 1 slash g')
                <=> (0,x) = (f y,g y) quad "for some" y in K_1 lr((D)) \
              & <=> x = g y quad "for some" y in ker f.
$
An analogous result holds for the case where $x in K_0 lr((A))$. Put
$ K_i lr((A,J)) = lr({y in K_i lr((D)): f(y) = 0}), quad i = 0,1. $
<eq:relative-k-groups-double-definition>
#idx("Relative K₁-group", target: [@eq:relative-k-groups-double-definition])
#idx("Relative K-theory", target: [@eq:relative-k-groups-double-definition])
We have thus shown that
$
  K_i lr((A,J)) arrow.r^g K_i lr((A)) arrow.r^(g')
  K_i lr((overline(A))), quad i = 0,1,
$
is exact.

#source(143)
Since $(f,g) dot (im partial) = 0$, it follows readily that there is an exact
sequence
$
  K_1 lr((A,J)) arrow.r^g K_1 lr((A)) arrow.r^(g')
  K_1 lr((overline(A))) arrow.r^partial K_0 lr((A,J))
  arrow.r^g K_0 lr((A)) arrow.r^(g') K_0 lr((overline(A))),
$
<eq:relative-double-exact-sequence>
as desired.

#remarks[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [When $J = A$ we obtain $overline(A) = 0$, $D = A times A$, and then
      $ K_i lr((A,A)) tilde.equiv K_i lr((A)), quad i = 0,1. $
      At the other extreme where $J = 0$, we get $D = A$, and then
      $K_i lr((A,0)) = 0$, $i = 0,1$.],
    [Let $Delta: A -> D$ be the diagonal map $Delta(a) = (a,a)$, $a in A$. Then
      $Delta$ splits the surjection $f: D -> A$, so the sequence
      $ 0 -> K_0 lr((A,J)) -> K_0 lr((D)) arrow.r^f K_0 lr((A)) -> 0 $
      is split exact. (Indeed, $Delta$ induces a splitting
      $K_i lr((A)) -> K_i lr((D))$.) This shows that
      $ K_0 lr((D)) tilde.equiv K_0 lr((A)) plus.o K_0 lr((A,J)). $
      In the same manner, we obtain
      $ K_1 lr((D)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((A)) times K_1 lr((A,J)). $],
  )
] <rem:relative-double-splittings>

We leave it as an exercise to the reader to establish an isomorphism between
this version of $K_0 lr((A,J))$ and the preceding version. However, we shall now
prove that the two definitions of $K_1 lr((A,J))$ agree.

#proposition[
  Let $J$ be a two-sided ideal of $A$, and let $D$ be the double of $A$ along
  $J$, as in @eq:relative-double-fiber-product. Set
  $ K_1^ast lr((A,J)) = lr({y in K_1 lr((D)): f(y) = 0}), $
  where $f: D -> A$. Then there is an isomorphism
  $ K_1^ast lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((A,J)), $
  where, as in @eq:relative-whitehead-group,
  $ K_1 lr((A,J)) = GL lr((A,J)) slash E lr((A,J)). $
] <prop:relative-whitehead-double-isomorphism>

#proof[
  From @eq:relative-double-fiber-product we obtain a fiber product of groups

  #source(144)
  #relative-double-general-linear
  where $f$ is viewed as a projection onto the first component. Clearly
  $ ker f = 1 times GL lr((A,J)). $
  Next, the surjection $f: D -> A$ induces a surjection
  $E lr((D)) -> E lr((A))$, also denoted by $f$. We shall prove that the
  sequence of groups
  $ 1 -> 1 times E lr((A,J)) -> E lr((D)) arrow.r^f E lr((A)) -> 1 $
  <eq:double-elementary-kernel-sequence>
  is exact. By @eq:relative-elementary-subgroup, $E lr((A,J))$ is the subgroup
  of $E lr((A))$ generated by all expressions $S T S^(-1)$, $S in E lr((A))$,
  where $T$ ranges over all elementary matrices for which $overline(T) = 1$ in
  $E lr((overline(A)))$. Now
  $ (1,S T S^(-1)) = (S,S)(1,T)(S,S)^(-1) "in" GL lr((D)), $
  and $(1,T) in E lr((D))$, so $(1,S T S^(-1)) in E lr((D))$. Thus,
  $1 times E lr((A,J))$ is a subgroup of $E lr((D))$ contained in $ker f$, and
  it remains for us to prove the reverse inclusion.

  Each element of $E lr((D))$ in $ker f$ must be of the form $(1,X)$, with
  $X in E lr((A))$ and $overline(X) = 1$ in $E lr((overline(A)))$. On the other
  hand, every element of $E lr((D))$ is a product of elementary matrices over
  $D$, and each such elementary matrix is of the form
  $
    (E_(i j) lr((r)),E_(i j) lr((s))), quad r,s in A, quad
    r equiv s (mod J).
  $
  We may thus write
  $ (1,X) = product_(i=1)^k (S_i,S_i T_i) "in" E lr((D)), $
  where for each $i$, $S_i,T_i$ are elementary matrices over $A$ and
  $overline(T_i) = 1$. Then $product S_i = 1$, and
  $
    X = product_(i=1)^k (S_i T_i)
    = (S_1 T_1 S_1^(-1))(S_1 S_2 T_2 lr((S_1 S_2))^(-1)) dots
    (S_1 dots S_k T_k lr((S_1 dots S_k))^(-1)).
  $
  The right-hand expression lies in $E lr((A,J))$ by the preceding remarks, so
  $(1,X) in 1 times E lr((A,J))$, and we have proved the exactness of
  @eq:double-elementary-kernel-sequence.

  #source(145)
  Now consider the commutative diagram with exact rows and columns:
  #relative-double-kernel-comparison
  Here, the $lr({f_i})$ are induced by $f$, while the $g_i$ arise from the map
  $g(x) = (1,x)$, $x in GL lr((A,J))$. The last column is the split exact
  sequence described in Remark @rem:relative-double-splittings (ii). The Remark
  also shows that both $f_1$ and $f_2$ are surjective. The Snake Lemma for
  Groups (Exercise @exc:snake-lemma-groups) then gives an exact sequence
  $ 1 -> E lr((A,J)) -> GL lr((A,J)) -> K_1^ast lr((A,J)) -> 1 $
  so $K_1 lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv K_1^ast lr((A,J))$, and the proposition is
  proved.
]

#remark(numbered: true)[
  It is natural to ask whether the analogue of the Excision Theorem is valid for
  $K_1$ as well as for $K_0$. In other words, under the hypotheses of
  @th:relative-grothendieck-excision, is there an isomorphism
  $K_1 lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((B,J))$? As shown by Swan @bib:Swan1971,
  this need not be the case in general. It does hold under suitable hypotheses,
  however; see @lem:relative-whitehead-excision.
] <rem:relative-whitehead-excision>
#idx("Excision Theorem", target: [@rem:relative-whitehead-excision])

We conclude this section by proving a theorem on injective stability for
$K_1 lr((A,J))$. Since $K_1 lr((A,A)) = K_1 lr((A))$, the result includes the
Bass–Vaserstein Theorem @th:injective-whitehead-stability as a special case. The
more general result will be vital in our discussion of $S K_1$ of orders (see
§@ss:sk1-finiteness, and especially the proof of
@th:order-inclusion-whitehead-finite-kernel-cokernel). The authors wish to thank
W. van der Kallen for supplying the following proof, which is adapted from work
of K. Dennis.

#theorem(title: [Injective Stability for Relative $K$-Theory])[
  Let $A$ be a ring with stable range $d$, and let $J$ be any two-sided ideal of
  $A$. Then for any $n >= d+1$, the following hold true:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$E_n lr((A,J))$ is a normal subgroup of $GL_n lr((A))$, and in fact
      $ E_n lr((A,J)) = GL_n lr((A)) inter E lr((A,J)). $
      <eq:relative-elementary-stable-intersection>],
    [The homomorphism $GL_n lr((A,J)) -> K_1 lr((A,J))$ induces an isomorphism
      $ GL_n lr((A,J)) slash E_n lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((A,J)). $
      <eq:relative-whitehead-stable-quotient>],
    [In the “absolute” case where $A = J$, we have
      $
        E_n lr((A)) = GL_n lr((A)) inter E lr((A)) quad "and" quad
        GL_n lr((A)) slash E_n lr((A)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((A)).
      $
      <eq:absolute-whitehead-stable-quotient>],
  )
] <th:relative-whitehead-injective-stability>
#idx("Stability theorems", target: [@th:relative-whitehead-injective-stability])
#idx(
  "Injective stability",
  target: [@th:relative-whitehead-injective-stability],
)

#source(146)
#proof[
  _Step 1._ We begin by showing that the relative case can be deduced from the
  absolute case. Suppose for the moment that (iii) has been proved, and let us
  deduce assertions (i) and (ii). Let $D$ be the double of $A$ along $J$. As
  will be shown in @lem:double-stable-range below, $D$ also has stable range
  $d$.

  Now let $n >= d+1$; the proof of @prop:relative-whitehead-double-isomorphism
  shows that the sequence
  $ 1 -> E_n lr((A,J)) arrow.r^g E_n lr((D)) arrow.r^f E_n lr((A)) -> 1 $
  <eq:finite-double-elementary-kernel-sequence>
  is exact, and also that there is a commutative diagram with exact rows:
  #relative-finite-general-linear-comparison
  In order to prove (i), we observe that $E lr((A,J)) ⊴ GL lr((A))$, so it
  suffices to establish @eq:relative-elementary-stable-intersection.
  Furthermore, we need only show that
  $GL_n lr((A)) inter E lr((A,J)) subset.eq E_n lr((A,J))$, the reverse
  inclusion being obvious. Given any $x in GL_n lr((A)) inter E lr((A,J))$, the
  map $g$ defined in @eq:finite-double-elementary-kernel-sequence carries $x$
  onto $g(x) in GL_n lr((D)) inter E lr((D))$. By virtue of (iii), applied to
  the ring $D$ rather than $A$, we deduce that $g(x) in E_n lr((D))$. But then
  $x in E_n lr((A,J))$ as desired, by the exactness of
  @eq:finite-double-elementary-kernel-sequence.

  We have thus shown (assuming (iii)) that $E_n lr((A,J)) ⊴ GL_n lr((A,J))$, and
  so we obtain a commutative diagram with exact rows
  #relative-finite-quotient-comparison
  By @th:surjective-whitehead-stability, both $beta$ and $gamma$ are surjective.
  It then follows from the Snake Lemma for Groups (see Exercise
  @exc:snake-lemma-groups) that if $beta$ and $gamma$ are isomorphisms, then so
  is $alpha$. Thus, (ii) follows from (iii), as claimed.

  _Step 2._ We now begin the proof of (iii). Since $GL_n lr((A)) -> K_1 lr((A))$
  is a surjection with kernel $GL_n lr((A)) inter E lr((A))$, we need only prove
  the first part of @eq:absolute-whitehead-stable-quotient. Further,
  $E lr((A)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r E_m lr((A))$, and thus it suffices to show
  $
    GL_n lr((A)) inter E_(n+1) lr((A)) = E_n lr((A))
    quad "for" n >= d+1.
  $
  <eq:elementary-successive-intersection>
  For the remainder of the proof, write $GL_n$ and $E_n$ in place of
  $GL_n lr((A))$ and $E_n lr((A))$, respectively. Let $n >= d+1$. An element
  $bold(x) in GL_(n+1)$ is said to be in _normal form_ if it is expressed as a
  product
  $
    bold(x) = mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(I)_n, 0; bold(b), 1) mat(
      bold(I)_n,
      bold(c); 0, 1
    )
    mat(1, 0; bold(d), bold(I)_n) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q)), quad "with"
    bold(P),bold(Q) in E_n,
  $
  <eq:elementary-stability-normal-form>
  where $0,bold(b),bold(c),bold(d)$ represent row or column vectors over $A$ of
  length $n$. Note that the middle factors are upper or lower triangular
  matrices.

  #source(147)
  The normal form of $bold(x)$ is usually not unique, a fact which will be
  useful below.

  Let us set
  $
    cal(N) = lr({bold(x) in E_(n+1): bold(x) "can be written in normal form"}).
  $
  The object of this step is to show that $cal(N) = E_(n+1)$, that is, each
  $bold(x) in E_(n+1)$ has a normal form. For this, it suffices to prove that
  for each $bold(x) in E_(n+1)$ of the type
  @eq:elementary-stability-normal-form, and each generator $E_(i j) lr((a))$,
  $a in A$, the product $E_(i j) lr((a)) bold(x)$ also has a normal form.

  We shall use repeatedly the following formula: for $bold(P) in GL_r$,
  $bold(S) in GL_s$, we have
  $
    mat(bold(P), 0; 0, bold(S)) mat(bold(I)_r, bold(Q); 0, bold(I)_s) =
    mat(bold(P), bold(P) bold(Q); 0, bold(S))
    = mat(bold(I)_r, bold(Q)'; 0, bold(I)_s) mat(bold(P), 0; 0, bold(S)),
  $
  where $bold(P) bold(Q) = bold(Q)' bold(S)$. Thus, we can shift the factor
  $mat(bold(P), 0; 0, bold(S))$ from one side of
  $mat(bold(I), bold(Q); 0, bold(I))$ to the other side, by changing the corner
  matrix $bold(Q)$.

  Now consider
  $
    bold(x)' = E_(i j) lr((a)) bold(x)
    = E_(i j) lr((a)) mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(I), 0; bold(b), 1)
    mat(bold(I), bold(c); 0, 1)
    mat(1, 0; bold(d), bold(I)) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q)), quad bold(P),bold(Q) in
    E_n.
  $
  <eq:elementary-normal-form-generator-product>
  Here, $E_(i j) lr((a))$ is the $(n+1) times (n+1)$ matrix, obtained from
  $bold(I)_(n+1)$ by placing the element $a$ in position $(i,j)$, where
  $i != j$. If both $i <= n$ and $j <= n$, then
  $ E_(i j) lr((a)) mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) = mat(bold(P)', 0; 0, 1) $
  for some $bold(P)' in E_n$, so $bold(x)' in cal(N)$, as desired.

  Next, suppose that $i = n+1$; then
  $
    E_(i j) lr((a)) = mat(bold(I), 0; bold(e)_j a, 1),
    quad "where" bold(e)_j = (0 dots 1 quad 0 dots 0).
  $
  Therefore
  $
    E_(i j) lr((a)) mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(I), 0; bold(b), 1)
    &= mat(bold(I), 0; bold(e)_j a, 1) mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) mat(
      bold(I), 0;
      bold(b), 1
    ) \
    &= mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(I), 0; bold(u), 1) mat(
      bold(I), 0;
      bold(b), 1
    )
    = mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(I), 0; bold(b)', 1)
  $
  for some $bold(u)$ and $bold(b)'$, so again $bold(x)' in cal(N)$.

  #source(148)
  We are left with the most difficult case, that where $j = n+1$. Here we have
  $
    E_(i j) lr((a)) = mat(bold(I)_n, bold(e) a; 0, 1),
    quad "where" bold(e) = attach((0 dots 1 quad 0 dots 0), tl: t).
  $
  Then
  $
    E_(i j) lr((a)) mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) = mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) mat(
      bold(I),
      bold(v) a; 0, 1
    ),
  $
  where $bold(v) = bold(P)^(-1) bold(e)$ is unimodular. Since $n >= d+1$ and $A$
  has stable range $d$, we can find a column vector $bold(w)$ of length $n-1$
  such that
  $
    mat(1, 0; -bold(w), bold(I)_(n-1)) bold(v) = bold(p) =
    attach((bold(p)_1,dots,bold(p)_n), tl: t),
  $
  where $attach((bold(p)_2,dots,bold(p)_n), tl: t)$ is unimodular. Now let
  $bold(X) = mat(1, 0; bold(w), bold(I))$; then
  $
    mat(1, 0; bold(d), bold(I)) = mat(bold(X), 0; 0, 1) mat(
      1, 0; bold(d)_1,
      bold(I)
    )
  $
  for some $bold(d)_1$. Making this substitution in
  @eq:elementary-normal-form-generator-product, we may then push the factor
  $mat(bold(X), 0; 0, 1)$ to the left and absorb it into
  $mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1)$, obtaining
  $
    bold(x)' = mat(bold(P)_1, 0; 0, 1) mat(
      bold(I)_n, bold(X)^(-1) bold(v) a; 0,
      1
    ) mat(bold(I), 0; bold(b)_1, 1)
    mat(bold(I), bold(c)_1; 0, 1) mat(1, 0; bold(d)_1, bold(I)) mat(
      1, 0; 0,
      bold(Q)
    )
  $
  for some $bold(P)_1 in E_n$, and some vectors $bold(b)_1,bold(c)_1$, and
  $bold(d)_1$. Now $bold(X)^(-1) bold(v) a = bold(p) a$, where $bold(p)$ is a
  column vector with $n$ components, whose lower $n-1$ components form a
  unimodular vector. In the above expression for $bold(x)'$, we replace the
  first two factors by
  $
    mat(bold(P)_2, 0; 0, 1) mat(1, bold(q), 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), 0; 0, 0, 1)
    mat(bold(I)_n, bold(X)^(-1) bold(v) a; 0, 1)
  $
  for some $bold(P)_2 in E_n$ and some vector $bold(q)$. By the preceding
  remarks, we may choose $bold(q)$ so that the top entry of the column vector
  $ mat(1, bold(q); 0, bold(I)_(n-1)) bold(X)^(-1) bold(v) a $
  is $0$.

  #source(149)
  We may then write
  $
    bold(x)' = mat(bold(P)_2, 0; 0, 1) mat(
      1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), bold(r);
      0, 0, 1
    )
    mat(1, bold(q), 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), 0; 0, 0, 1) mat(
      bold(I), 0; bold(b)_1,
      1
    )
    mat(bold(I), bold(c)_1; 0, 1) mat(1, 0; bold(d)_1, bold(I)) mat(
      1, 0; 0,
      bold(Q)
    ).
  $
  The product of the middle three factors can be written as
  $
    mat(bold(I)_n, 0; bold(b)_2, 1) mat(
      1, bold(q), 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), 0; 0,
      0, 1
    ) mat(bold(I)_n, bold(c)_1; 0, 1)
    = mat(bold(I)_n, 0; bold(b)_2, 1) mat(1, bold(c)_2; 0, bold(I)_n) mat(
      1, 0;
      0, bold(U)
    )
  $
  for some vectors $bold(b)_2,bold(c)_2$, and some $bold(U) in E_n$. The factor
  $mat(1, 0; 0, bold(U))$ can be pushed to the right and absorbed into the last
  factor of $bold(x)'$, to give
  $
    bold(x)' = mat(bold(P)_2, 0; 0, 1) mat(
      1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), bold(r);
      0, 0, 1
    )
    mat(bold(I)_n, 0; bold(b)_2, 1) mat(1, bold(c)_2; 0, bold(I)_n) mat(
      1, 0;
      bold(d)_2, bold(I)_n
    ) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q)_1)
  $
  for some vector $bold(d)_2$ and some $bold(Q)_1 in E_n$.

  Now $n >= 2$, so we may write
  $
    mat(bold(I)_n, 0; bold(b)_2, 1) = mat(
      1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), 0; s,
      bold(y), 1
    )
    = mat(1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), 0; s, 0, 1) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(V)),
  $
  where
  $ bold(V) = mat(bold(I)_(n-1), 0; bold(y), 1) in E_n, $
  and where $s$ is scalar, $bold(y)$ a vector. Pushing the factor
  $mat(1, 0; 0, bold(V))$ to the right, we obtain
  $
    bold(x)' = mat(bold(P)_2, 0; 0, 1) mat(
      1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), bold(r);
      0, 0, 1
    )
    mat(1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I)_(n-1), 0; s, 0, 1) mat(1, bold(c)_3; 0, bold(I))
    mat(1, 0; bold(d)_3, bold(I)) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q)_2).
  $
  However, we have
  $
    mat(1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I), bold(r); 0, 0, 1) mat(
      1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I), 0; s, 0,
      1
    )
    = mat(1, 0, 0; bold(r) s, bold(I), 0; 0, 0, 1) mat(
      1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I), 0;
      s, 0, 1
    )
    mat(1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I), bold(r); 0, 0, 1).
  $

  #source(150)
  Substitute into the formula for $bold(x)'$, and write
  $
    mat(bold(P)_2, 0; 0, 1) mat(1, 0, 0; bold(r) s, bold(I), 0; 0, 0, 1)
    = mat(bold(P)_3, 0; 0, 1), quad "where" bold(P)_3 in E_n.
  $
  Then
  $
    bold(x)' & = mat(bold(P)_3, 0; 0, 1) mat(1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I), 0; s, 0, 1)
               mat(
                 1, 0, 0;
                 0, bold(I), bold(r);
                 0, 0, 1
               ) mat(1, bold(c)_3; 0, bold(I))
               mat(1, 0; bold(d)_3, bold(I)) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q)_2) \
             & = mat(bold(P)_3, 0; 0, 1) mat(1, 0, 0; 0, bold(I), 0; s, 0, 1)
               mat(1, bold(c)_4; 0, bold(I)) mat(1, 0; bold(d)_4, bold(I)) mat(
                 1, 0; 0,
                 bold(Q)_3
               ),
  $
  for some $bold(c)_4,bold(d)_4$ and some $bold(Q)_3 in E_n$.

  Finally, we have
  $
    mat(1, bold(c)_4; 0, bold(I)) = mat(bold(W), 0; 0, 1) mat(
      bold(I), bold(f);
      0, 1
    )
  $
  for some $bold(W) in E_n$ and some vector $bold(f)$. Pushing the factor
  $mat(bold(W), 0; 0, 1)$ to the left, we obtain
  $
    bold(x)' = mat(bold(P)_4, 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(I), 0; bold(t), 1) mat(
      bold(I),
      bold(f); 0, 1
    )
    mat(1, 0; bold(d)_4, bold(I)) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q)_3)
  $
  with $bold(P)_4 in E_n$. This shows that $bold(x)'$ can be written in normal
  form. We have thus proved that every vector $bold(x) in E_(n+1)$ can be
  written in the normal form @eq:elementary-stability-normal-form.

  _Step 3._ We now prove @eq:elementary-successive-intersection, so let
  $n >= d+1$ and let $bold(X) in GL_n lr((A))$ be such that
  $mat(bold(X), 0; 0, 1) in E_(n+1) lr((A))$. We must prove that
  $bold(X) in E_n lr((A))$. By Step 2, we may write
  $
    mat(bold(X), 0; 0, 1) = mat(bold(P), 0; 0, 1) mat(bold(I)_n, 0; bold(b), 1)
    mat(bold(I)_n, bold(c); 0, 1)
    mat(1, 0; bold(d), bold(I)_n) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q))
  $
  with $bold(P),bold(Q) in E_n lr((A))$. Replacing $bold(X)$ by
  $bold(P)^(-1) bold(X)$ and changing notation, we may take
  $bold(P) = bold(I)_n$ for the rest of the proof. Next,
  $
    mat(1, 0; bold(d), bold(I)_n) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q)) = mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q))
    mat(1, 0; bold(d)', bold(I)_n)
  $
  for some $bold(d)'$, so we obtain
  $
    mat(bold(I)_n, 0; -bold(b), 1) mat(bold(X), 0; 0, 1) mat(
      1, 0; -bold(d)',
      bold(I)_n
    )
    = mat(bold(I)_n, bold(c); 0, 1) mat(1, 0; 0, bold(Q)).
  $

  #source(151)
  This gives
  $ mat(bold(X)', 0; bold(b)', 1) = mat(1, bold(e); 0, bold(Q)') $
  for some new matrices $bold(X)',bold(Q)'$ and vectors $bold(b)',bold(e)$,
  where
  $ bold(X)' equiv bold(X), bold(Q)' equiv bold(Q) quad mod E_n lr((A)). $
  Comparing the above two matrices, we obtain
  $
    mat(bold(X)', 0; bold(b)', 1) = mat(
      1, bold(u), 0; 0, bold(M), 0; 0,
      bold(v), 1
    ) = mat(1, bold(e); 0, bold(Q)'),
  $
  for some vectors $bold(u),bold(v)$ and some matrix $bold(M)$. Then
  $
    mat(bold(M), 0; 0, 1) = mat(bold(M), 0; bold(v), 1) mat(
      bold(I), 0;
      -bold(v), 1
    )
    = bold(Q)' mat(bold(I), 0; -bold(v), 1) in E_n lr((A)).
  $
  Also,
  $
    bold(X)' = mat(1, bold(u); 0, bold(M)) = mat(1, 0; 0, bold(M)) mat(
      1,
      bold(u); 0, bold(I)
    ).
  $
  But
  $ mat(1, 0; 0, bold(M)) in E_n lr((A)), $
  since $mat(bold(M), 0; 0, 1) in E_n lr((A))$, and $E_n lr((A))$ is invariant
  under conjugation by permutation matrices. It follows that
  $bold(X) in E_n lr((A))$, which completes the proof of the theorem.
]

It remains for us to verify the following result, whose proof was also supplied
by W. van der Kallen.

#lemma[
  Let $D$ be the double of $A$ along $J$, and suppose that $A$ has stable range
  $d$. Then $D$ also has stable range $d$.
] <lem:double-stable-range>

#proof[
  Let $n >= d+1$, and let
  $ bold(v) = [(a_1,b_1),dots,(a_n,b_n)] in D^((n)) $
  be a unimodular row over $D$. We must show that by subtracting left multiples
  of the last entry $(a_n,b_n)$ from the first $(n-1)$ components of $bold(v)$,
  we can make the first $n-1$ components into a unimodular row. Call this
  process “reduction.”

  Since $(a_1,dots,a_n)$ is unimodular over $A$, and $n >= d+1$, there exist
  elements $lr({x_i})$ from $A$ for which

  #source(152)
  $ (a_1+x_1 a_n,a_2+x_2 a_n,dots,a_(n-1)+x_(n-1) a_n) $
  is unimodular. Put
  $ c_i = a_i+x_i a_n, quad d_i = b_i+x_i b_n, quad 1 <= i <= n-1. $
  Then the row
  $ bold(w) = [(c_1,d_1),dots,(c_(n-1),d_(n-1)),(a_n,b_n)] in D^((n)) $
  is unimodular, and comes from $bold(v)$ by “reduction,” so it suffices to show
  how to “reduce” $bold(w)$. Now $(c_1,dots,c_(n-1))$ is unimodular, so we may
  choose $lr({y_i})$ from $A$ so that $sum_(i=1)^(n-1) y_i c_i = 1$, and then
  $ sum_(i=1)^(n-1) y_i d_i = 1+x quad "for some" x in J. $
  Let us write
  $ S = sum_(i=1)^(n-1) D(c_i,d_i), $
  a left ideal of $D$ containing $(1,1+x)$. Since $bold(w)$ is unimodular, there
  exists $(t,u) in D$ such that
  $ (1,1) equiv (t,u)(a_n,b_n) (mod S). $
  Multiplying on the left by $(0,x) in D$, we obtain
  $ (0,x) equiv (0,x u b_n) (mod S), $
  and therefore
  $ (1,1) = (1,1+x)-(0,x) equiv (0,-x u b_n) (mod S). $
  But then
  $ bold(y) = [(c_1,d_1),dots,(c_(n-1),d_(n-1)),(0,-x u b_n)] in D^((n)) $
  is unimodular, and since $(0,-x u b_n)$ is a left multiple of $(a_n,b_n)$, it
  suffices to “reduce” the vector $bold(y)$. Subtracting suitable left multiples
  of $(0,-x u b_n)$ from the first $n-1$ components of $bold(y)$, we obtain a
  new vector
  $ bold(z) = [(c_1,e_1),dots,(c_(n-1),e_(n-1)),(0,-x u b_n)] $
  in which $(e_1,dots,e_(n-1)) in A^((n-1))$ is unimodular.

  #source(153)
  Now let
  $ S' = sum_(i=1)^(n-1) D(c_i,e_i). $
  Then there exist $lr({g_i}) in A$ with
  $
    sum_(i=1)^(n-1) g_i c_i = 1, quad
    sum_(i=1)^(n-1) g_i e_i = 1+x',
  $
  with $x' in J$. Then $(1,1+x') in S'$. But $S'$ contains an element $(ast,1)$,
  so multiplying on the left by $(0,x')$, we see that $(0,x') in S'$. Therefore
  $(1,1) in S'$, which shows that the first $n-1$ components of $bold(z)$ form a
  unimodular row. This completes the proof of the lemma.
]

For future reference, we list a special case of Theorem
@th:relative-whitehead-injective-stability:

#corollary[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [Let $R$ be a Dedekind domain, and let $A$ be an $R$-algebra, f.g./$R$ as
      module. Let $J$ be any two-sided ideal of $A$. Then for $n >= 3$ there is
      an isomorphism
      $ GL_n lr((A,J)) slash E_n lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((A,J)), $
      induced by the surjection $GL_n lr((A,J)) -> K_1 lr((A,J))$.],
    [More generally, let $R$ be a commutative noetherian ring of Krull dimension
      $d$. Then the above isomorphism holds whenever $n >= d+2$.],
  )
] <cor:relative-whitehead-finite-algebra-stability>
#idx(
  "Stability theorems",
  target: [@cor:relative-whitehead-finite-algebra-stability],
)

The result is immediate from @th:relative-whitehead-injective-stability, by
virtue of the “surjective stability” theorems @cor:surjective-stability-dedekind
and @th:bass-stable-range-finite-dimension.

#exercises-heading

#exercise[
  Let $A$ be a commutative ring, and let $K_0 lr((A))$ be made into a ring with
  multiplication defined via $times.o_A$ (see @prop:lattice-grothendieck-ring
  with $G = 1$). Then for the ring $D$ defined by
  @eq:relative-double-fiber-product, $K_0 lr((D))$ is also a commutative ring.
  Show that the map $f_*: K_0 lr((D)) -> K_0 lr((A))$ induced by $f$ is a ring
  homomorphism. Prove further that the diagonal map $Delta: A -> D$ induces a
  ring homomorphism $Delta_*$ which splits $f_*$. Using the exact sequence
  $ 0 -> K_0 lr((A,J)) -> K_0 lr((D)) arrow.r^(f_*) K_0 lr((A)) -> 0, $
  deduce that $K_0 lr((A,J))$ is a module over the ring $K_0 lr((A))$.
] <exc:relative-grothendieck-module>

#exercise[
  Keep the above notation. Show that $K_1 lr((A,J))$ is a $K_0 lr((A))$-module,
  where each $x in K_0 lr((A))$ acts as $Delta_* lr((x))$, using the action of
  $K_0 lr((D))$ on $K_1 lr((D))$ (see Exercise @exc:whitehead-frobenius-module
  with $G = 1$).

  Show also that there is a pairing
  $ K_0 lr((A,J)) times K_1 lr((A)) -> K_1 lr((A,J)), $
  given by
  $
    (x,y) mapsto x dot Delta_* y, quad "where"
    Delta_*: K_1 lr((A)) -> K_1 lr((D)).
  $
] <exc:relative-whitehead-module-pairing>

#source(154)
#exercise[
  *(Snake Lemma for Groups.)* Let
  #relative-snake-groups
  be a commutative diagram of groups, with exact rows, and assume that
  $im rho ⊴ B$, $im alpha ⊴ A'$, and so on. Then there is an exact sequence of
  groups
  $
    ker alpha arrow.r^(rho_*) ker beta -> ker gamma -> op("cok") alpha
    -> op("cok") beta arrow.r^(sigma_*) op("cok") gamma.
  $
  Further, if $rho$ is surjective, so is $rho_*$; if $sigma$ is surjective, so
  is $sigma_*$.
] <exc:snake-lemma-groups>
#idx("Snake Lemma for groups", target: [@exc:snake-lemma-groups])
